0024-gpui-webview-appearance.patch (2026-11-15, rebuilt and compiled). Replaces the 2026-11-15 draft of the same
name (now in nsh-host-patches/superseded-2026-11-13/): that version ended in `call.reject("...not implemented")`
and depended on 0022's own draft registry, which this rebuild does not have (see 0022's new header: `partition`
is accepted but not scoped to any per-partition store). This version was built against the real tree
(third_party/nsh-host-build-0022) and a release binary was produced from it.

Applies with `patch -p1` at the root of a copy of nsh-host-allspeech-src after patches 0010-0023, in order.
Verified: applies cleanly on top of 0023, and `cargo build --release --locked --offline` of `nsh-gpui-host`
succeeds with no new Cargo.lock entries.

gpui: webview appearance (colour-scheme) override via NSResponder's setAppearance:

Why: DESKTOP_PARITY_AUDIT.md's "Preview: ... colour scheme only for server tabs" item — a native WKWebView tab
has no equivalent of the reference's per-tab `Emulation.setEmulatedMedia` CDP call, because NSH exposes no CDP
debugger surface for a WKWebView.

Changed from the original draft's plan: the draft said this would call `WKWebView.overrideUserInterfaceStyle`.
That property is macOS 13.3+ only and this tree has no typed WebKit binding for it without adding the
`objc2-web-kit` crate (a new Cargo.lock entry this patch avoids, matching 0022's and 0015's convention). Since
`WKWebView` is an `NSView`, `NSResponder`'s `setAppearance:` (nil for "system", an `NSAppearance` named
`NSAppearanceNameAqua`/`NSAppearanceNameDarkAqua` for "light"/"dark") reaches the same `prefers-color-scheme`
effect through AppKit's effective-appearance machinery and works on every macOS version this host ships on.

Known divergence from the reference, carried over from 0022's decision (document, do not silently narrow away):
CDP's `Emulation.setEmulatedMedia` is scoped to one tab; this host has no per-view `host.call` addressing
(`webview.capture`, patch 0015, addresses a view by on-screen point, not a stable id) and no per-partition
`WKWebsiteDataStore` (0022's header). So `webview.appearance.set { partition, scheme }` ignores `partition` and
applies `scheme` to every `WKWebView` shown right now (`native/web.rs`'s `shown()`, the same registry
`webview.capture` and `webview.appearance.set`'s own module doc describe); a tab opened afterwards starts back at
"system", since nothing here stores a default for web views built later.

Built/verified: `cargo check`/`cargo clippy` (clean) and a release build, both `--locked --offline`; a headless
`nsh test` smoke run got `webview.appearance.set` resolving `null` for both "dark" and "system" and
`desktop.capabilities` listing the name. NOT verified: whether `setAppearance:` actually flips a loaded page's
`prefers-color-scheme` media query result (no WKWebView was mounted in the smoke test) — if it does not, the
right fix is `overrideUserInterfaceStyle` via a typed WebKit binding (a real, larger change this patch avoided
for the Cargo.lock reason above), not another `setAppearance:` variant.

